osslscope-service capacity notes (Debian)
------------------------------------------

The continuous observer shares one 256 MiB watch pool across its
sessions and ELF parses. A maximal session reserves 122,990,172 B
(pinned by the tight reservation test with production object
sizes); two maximal sessions leave about 21 MiB, while a fresh
uncached parse needs a 22 MiB transient. Two maximal sessions plus
a fresh parse therefore do not fit; the run-shared ParseCache
(Stamp-keyed on dev, inode, size, mode, links, mtime and ctime; at
most 16 distinct files, first-fill wins) lets later sessions reuse
already-parsed offsets without the transient when the working set
is stable and within cap.

A cache miss falls back to the uncached parse, which can then fail
with a StateLimit/ReadLimit gap when the pool is contended. The
miss path is honest loss accounting, not equivalent availability:

- up to 16 distinct stable files: hits fit (demonstrated
  reservation/cache property only: an admitted same-file session
  reuses cached offsets without the transient, inside the pinned
  reservation; admission proves one stock parse transient per
  session until a member attaches, then the lease alone
  (attached members' parses are cached for followers to
  clone); members beyond pool capacity refuse with an
  explicit `global_state_limit` gap instead of starving live
  members; runtime multi-worker observation is not yet
  qualified and is not claimed here);
- cache full: further distinct files always miss;
- churning Stamps (upgrades, container layer turnover): new
  Stamps miss until filled if room remains, otherwise always;
- 17 or more distinct libcrypto builds: over cap; misses may gap.

Gaps are reported in the evidence; refused scopes are not
qualified. The cache alone does not restore a two-session
guarantee for churning or over-cap working sets. Narrow the
observed scope or stabilize the image set and re-qualify.

See docs/install.md ("Watch capacity and ParseCache") and
docs/overhead-matrix.md for the full arithmetic.
